00:34:32.800 --> 00:34:38.920
the most pulled me aside and said, listen, this can be a blip in your

00:34:38.920 --> 00:34:44.000
life, or it can be the defining event in your life. Make it the blip.

00:34:45.000 --> 00:34:56.000
And so I took the deal. It's funny that the deal, the case itself, made me far

00:34:56.000 --> 00:35:01.000
better known than I ever would have been before. I mean, when I blew the whistle

00:35:01.000 --> 00:35:06.000
on the torture program, it was a story for a few days a week. But my

00:35:06.000 --> 00:35:13.000
prosecution made me a martyr for human rights. It gave me a venue

00:35:14.000 --> 00:35:19.000
a soapbox with which I could speak to the American people. I'm on TV all the

00:35:19.000 --> 00:35:26.000
time. Thanks to these idiots at the Justice Department. Otherwise, nobody

00:35:26.000 --> 00:35:30.000
would even remember that there was a guy who said anything about the

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:35.000
torture program. I was very pleased one day when I was sitting in

00:35:35.000 --> 00:35:44.000
prison. It was it was December of 2014. And I was reading a four day old copy

00:35:44.000 --> 00:35:47.000
of the New York Times. They would of course, go through my New York

00:35:47.000 --> 00:35:51.000
